  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "If $Z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, then ${Z}^{4}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, to find ${Z}^{2},$ we multiply $Z$ by itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{2} & =Z\\cdot Z \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${Z}^{4}={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Z}^{4} & ={Z}^{2}\\cdot {Z}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}38 & 18 \\\\ 9 & 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
